        "content": "{\n  \"quantitative_comparisons\": [\n    {\n      \"research_object\": \"inosine supplementation in neurodegenerative disease patients\",\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"serum urate concentration 6-7 mg/dL\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"up to 3 g/d for 2 years\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"inosine treatment group\",\n          \"value\": \"6-7 mg/dL serum urate\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"research_object\": \"inosine vs adenosine half-life\",\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"inosine 15 h, adenosine ~5 s\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"pharmacokinetic comparison\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"inosine\",\n          \"value\": \"15 h\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"group\": \"adenosine\",\n          \"value\": \"~5 s\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"research_object\": \"solid tumor patients on anti-PD-1 therapy\",\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"fecal SCFA association with PFS\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"52 patients, nivolumab or pembrolizumab\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"higher fecal acetate/propionate/butyrate/valerate\",\n          \"value\": \"longer progression-free survival\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"research_object\": \"NSCLC patients on second-line nivolumab\",\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"fecal propionate and butyrate association\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"11 patients\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"propionate and butyrate\",\n          \"value\": \"significantly associated with long-term beneficial effects\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"research_object\": \"patients on anti-CTLA-4 (ipilimumab)\",\n      \"result_or_measure\": \"serum butyrate and propionate correlation\",\n      \"setting_or_time\": \"ICI-treated cohort\",\n      \"groups\": [\n        {\n          \"group\": \"high serum butyrate and propionate\",\n          \"value\": \"negatively correlated with ICI efficacy\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"usable_content\": \"Three recent reviews (2022, 2024, 2026) explicitly confirm that no published studies report absolute concentrations (μmol/g tissue or μM) of microbiome-derived metabolites measured directly in human tumour tissue or tumour interstitial fluid from ICI-treated patients correlated with clinical response. The 2024 inosine review states human inosine studies have been limited to urate-elevating properties in neurodegenerative diseases (serum urate 6-7 mg/dL at 3 g/d for 2 years), with animal model findings (B. pseudolongum-produced inosine enhancing ICI efficacy in mice) not yet translated to human tumor measurements. The 2022 metabolites-ICI review reports clinical correlations only from fecal samples (52 solid tumor patients on anti-PD-1: higher fecal acetate, propionate, butyrate, valerate associated with longer PFS; 11 NSCLC patients: fecal propionate and butyrate associated with benefit) and serum (high serum butyrate/propionate negatively correlated with anti-CTLA-4 efficacy), explicitly noting absence of absolute concentration thresholds for human TME. The 2026 NNS-tumor immunity review states paired measurements across stool, blood, tumor tissue, and tumor interstitial fluid are lacking in current literature, with inosine evidence restricted to preclinical mouse models. All three sources distinguish fecal/serum measurements (documented) from intratumoral measurements (unmet gap). Inosine's mechanistic rationale (A2A receptor signaling on tumor-infiltrating T lymphocytes, 15 h half-life vs adenosine ~5 s) is established from animal/cell studies but lacks human tumor concentration validation.\",\n  \"decision\": \"direct_use\",\n  \"still_missing\": \"\",\n  \"read_focus\": \"\",\n  \"external_ask\": \"\",\n  \"reason\": \"The question asks whether any published studies report absolute intratumoral metabolite concentrations correlated with ICI response. All three materials explicitly confirm this data is lacking, satisfying success criterion #2 (confirmation that such measurements are explicitly stated as lacking in systematic reviews or methodological papers). The reviews clearly distinguish fecal/serum data (available) from tumor tissue/interstitial fluid data (unavailable), providing direct evidence to answer the yes/no question with documented gap confirmation across multiple recent sources (2022-2026).\"\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user is asking a very specific question about whether any published studies report absolute concentrations (μmol/g tissue or μM) of microbiome-derived metabolites (inosine, short-chain fatty acids, bile acids) measured directly in human tumour tissue or tumour interstitial fluid from immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI)-treated patients, correlated with clinical response.\n\nThe success criteria require:\n1. Identification of specific studies with paired tumour tissue metabolomics and ICI outcome data, OR\n2. Confirmation that such measurements are explicitly stated as lacking in systematic reviews or methodological papers.\n3. Must distinguish between fecal/serum measurements (already documented) and intratumoral measurements (the gap).\n\nLet me analyze the material_found:\n\n1. **P0599 (2024 review on inosine)**: \n   - States human clinical trials used inosine to elevate serum urate in neurodegenerative diseases\n   - These studies did NOT measure tumor concentrations or ICI response\n   - Animal model data (Mager et al., 2020) shows inosine concentration was highest in duodenum and overabundant in sera of mice mono-colonized with B. pseudolongum\n   - Notes these findings have NOT yet been translated to human tumor measurements\n   - Explicitly states the gap: \"No quantitative data on intratumoral inosine levels in cancer patients correlating with ICI response is provided\"\n\n2. **P0413 (2022 review on gut metabolites and ICI)**:\n   - Reports clinical cohort data linking FECAL SCFA concentrations to ICI outcomes (52 solid tumor patients, 11 NSCLC patients)\n   - Higher fecal concentrations of acetate, propionate, butyrate, valerate associated with longer PFS in anti-PD-1 therapy\n   - High serum butyrate and propionate negatively correlated with ICI efficacy in anti-CTLA-4 patients\n   - For Inosine: describes role through preclinical mouse models, does NOT provide human physiological concentration data for Inosine in TME or serum linked to response\n   - Explicitly states: \"lacks specific quantitative concentration thresholds for human TME or paired serum samples for most metabolites beyond the general association with fecal SCFAs\"\n\n3. **P0589 (2026 review on NNS and tumor immunity)**:\n   - Explicitly states that paired measurements of candidate metabolites across stool, portal or peripheral blood, tumor tissue, and tumor interstitial fluid are LACKING in current literature\n   - For inosine: describes as proof-of-principle in mice, citing preclinical evidence rather than human tumor concentrations\n   - For SCFAs: reports quantitative data only from fecal samples in NNS intervention trials, without linking these fecal levels to human tumor microenvironment concentrations or ICI outcomes\n\nAll three sources consistently confirm that human tumor tissue/interstitial fluid metabolite concentrations correlated with ICI response are NOT available in published literature. The evidence is limited to fecal and serum measurements, with intratumoral data remaining a significant gap.\n\nNow I need to structure the quantitative_comparisons carefully. The sources provide some numerical data but mostly for fecal/serum measurements, not tumor tissue. I should extract what's available while being clear about the limitations.\n\nFrom P0413:\n- 52 solid tumor patients receiving anti-PD-1 therapy: higher fecal SCFAs associated with longer PFS (no absolute concentrations given)\n- 11 NSCLC patients: propionate and butyrate associated with long-term beneficial effects (no absolute concentrations)\n- Serum butyrate and propionate negatively correlated with anti-CTLA-4 efficacy (no absolute concentrations)\n\nFrom P0599:\n- Inosine supplementation up to 3 g/d for 2 years increased serum urate to 6-7 mg/dL (this is for neurodegenerative diseases, not ICI cancer patients)\n- Inosine half-life 15 h vs adenosine ~5 s\n\nFrom P0589:\n- No quantitative tumor tissue data provided\n- References fecal SCFA studies (Ahmad et al. 2020, Farup et al. 2019) but no absolute concentrations linked to ICI\n\nThe key finding is that NO studies provide absolute concentrations (μmol/g or μM) in human tumor tissue/interstitial fluid correlated with ICI response. This is explicitly confirmed by all three reviews.\n\nFor quantitative_comparisons, I should note what IS available (fecal/serum associations) while making clear these don't meet the success criteria. The usable_content should emphasize the explicit confirmation of the gap across multiple recent reviews.\n\nLet me structure this properly:\n\nquantitative_comparisons: I need to extract actual numbers from the text. Looking at P0599:\n- \"inosine ingestion up to 3 g/d for 2 years increased serum urate to an optimum concentration of 6-7 mg/dL\" - this is for neurodegenerative disease studies, not ICI cancer patients\n- \"inosine... significantly longer half-life (15 h) than adenosine (~5 s)\"\n\nFrom P0413:\n- \"cohort of 52 solid tumor patients receiving anti-PD-1 therapy\"\n- \"cohort of 11 non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients\"\n- No absolute concentration values provided for SCFAs in tumor tissue\n\nFrom P0589:\n- No quantitative tumor tissue data\n\nI should be careful not to fabricate numbers. The text doesn't provide absolute concentrations (μmol/g or μM) for tumor tissue metabolites. I should report what IS there (cohort sizes, serum urate levels from non-ICI studies) and make clear the tumor tissue concentration data is absent.\n\ndecision: This is actually direct_use for confirming the GAP exists. The question asks \"Do any published studies report...\" and the answer based on these materials is \"No, multiple recent reviews explicitly confirm this data is lacking.\" This satisfies success criterion #2 (confirmation that such measurements are explicitly stated as lacking in systematic reviews or methodological papers).\n\nHowever, I need to be careful: the question asks for a yes/no about whether studies exist. The materials confirm they don't exist (as of the review publication dates). This is direct_use because the reviews explicitly state the gap.\n\nLet me write the response carefully, distinguishing between what data exists (fecal/serum) vs what doesn't (tumor tissue).",
